    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1115">

                <pb facs="0311" n="2013" xml:id="pg2013"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">774.5/3–953: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 1115<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the
                        Embassy in Egypt</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d1115-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-03-09T16:29:00-05:00">March 9, 1953—4:29
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1781. Following is text telegram sent by Eden to Foreign Office after discussion
                    with Secretary referred to Deptel 1776 Mar 7.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1115-f2"
                    /></p>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Verbatim text</hi>. I have had further discussion with
                    Mr. Dulles on this point. We have agreed that the best procedure would be for
                    Sir Ralph Stevenson to call on the Egyptian Prime Minister or Minister for
                    Foreign Affairs, accompanied by his U.S. colleague, and explain to him the
                    reasons why it is proposed that the U.S. Government should be associated in the
                    negotiations. Mr. Caffery would confirm the willingess of the U.S. Government
                    that he should so participate.</p>
                <p>Sir Ralph Stevenson would base his argument broadly on the fact that these
                    negotiations will, we hope, lead up to a settlement of Middle East defense
                    problems and the building up of Egyptian economy, in which the U.S. Government
                    are bound to take a prominent part.</p>
                <p>No action should be taken on this telegram into Cairo pending further
                    instructions. <hi rendition="#italic">End verbatim text</hi>.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Smith</hi></hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1115-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d1115-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to London as telegram 5972. It was drafted by William C. Burdett,
                    Officer in Charge, Egypt and Anglo-Egyptian Sudan Affairs, and was approved by
                    Hart.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1115-f2" n="2" xml:id="d1115-f2-content">
